      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "As an alternative, if the above suggestion will not work because the code to do the viseme stuff has to be on the avatar itself... Add a list to the VRC script that can be expanded where you can drag and drop audio sources that should be treated as voice for the purpouses of viseme generation.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2019-12-02T17:05:53.526Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Having an avatar's mouth move when they're speaking or singing a song is something which makes VR more immersive. But currently the only easy way to do that is to play audio clips through your mic using various external tools. \n\nBut this is not ideal because it requires the user to have these tools installed along with the necessary audio clips, and it bypasses everyone's settings for avatar audio volume level because it's playing through the mic, which can be annoying.\n\nAnd while in theory one could record visemes into an animation for later playback in VRChat, this is quite complicated to set up, and requires a second copy of your avatar with a bunch of rigidbodies so you can still move while the face is being animated. Hardly ideal, or performant.\n\nHowever, I believe there exists a solution to this problem which should be fairly easy to implement. If you were to add a checkbox to the VRC_SpatialAudioSource script which tells it to interpret the audio just as it would audio coming through your mic, then it would be trivial to make your avatar look like it's singing or speaking various lines. \n\nIt would also be nice if when you have a song with the vocals isolated, you could play those on a muted audio source and they would still generate the visemes, while you play the full track on another unmuted channel, so that the mouth does not move with the instrumentals.",
